hi there steve kaufman here again i'm uh  going off skiing for a week to big white  near kelowna  so i probably won't be doing any more  videos for a while  so i thought i would just do one here on  this whole subject of  of language acquisition versus language  learning and so forth which seems to  have  generated a bit of discussion on my blog  i am  a great admirer  of krashen  he igor in macedonia says that crash and  didn't originate these theories whatever  i've come across them by a crash and  when i read a lot of things that he  wrote i said yeah exactly that's how i  learn  um  but i don't  accept everything that he says  and one of the things where i i disagree  and it's not an important point really  but it's worth talking about and i'll  explain why and this is this whole issue  of learning a language versus acquiring  a language  and he sort of  and and many of the people who  write about language learning  try to say that there are two different  approaches one is learning which is  serious and deliberate and learning  about the grammar and the other is  acquisition which is something that we  do naturally and some people think that  only  children acquire their first language  whereas thereafter it's a more  deliberate learning process but they  still therefore talk about a dichotomy  crash and suggest that we continue to be  in fact  acquiring the language rather than  learning it  to me  i don't like it i don't like the use of  the word acquire because what is the  choir if i say you acquire something  means you bought it you took it  and it then became yours and you put it  away somewhere  the word learn to me  simply means that the brain is learning  and i don't like the association of  learning with the classroom with school  with teachers  with exercises drills tests all of the  formal formalized  uh  ceremonies that we seem to have  attached to learning  to me learning is as described in the  book by manfred spitzer which i highly  recommend  it's called  learning  and the brain  or the school for life or something like  that you can look it up on amazon  our brain learns all the time  our brain learns by establishing  patterns  uh it's not just the left side of the  brain or the rational side of the brain  all different parts of the brain get  involved emotion gets involved and  through a process of repetition and and  this whole business of  neurons firing with each other and then  again and again and creating these  connections which form these patterns so  that the brain then after a while  doesn't have to rediscover everything  because it's got a lot of patterns in  place and this process of learning is  how we learn about experience how we  learn to deal with people how we learn  to walk so we don't fall down all the  things that we learn and  and as we get older we have more and  more of these patterns in place because  we've come across them more often  younger people are still discovering  things and they're starting to become  patterns  and so everything is learning  uh but learning doesn't mean that you  necessarily hold on to it it goes in  you might forget it you might know it  for a while and forget it  uh the brain might i don't know i'm not  a neurologist or from what i've read  might discard things that they don't the  brain doesn't come across too often and  yet for some strange reason the brain is  capable of remembering things that you  learned a long long time ago and don't  need anymore  so it's a as i said it's sort of a  random process whereby the brain tries  to sort through  all of the stimulus that it gets puts  labels on things develops patterns rules  this is what this is learning learning  is not sitting in a classroom learning  goes on all the time  so language learning  is the same whether it's a child  learning its first language or an adult  sure there are different circumstances  the child has no other language  to interfere  because when we already have our own  language we have a tendency  to go back to what is already hardwired  in our brain in terms of structures  pronunciation and so forth the child  doesn't have this interference on the  other hand an older person learning they  have their existing vocabulary their  life experience  which helps them learn in a different  way but it's still learning and i don't  accept any distinction between  acquisition and learning to me it's all  learning  so that's one point by the same token  this learning is a natural process but  crashing to my mind that some of the  ardent believers in his approach  including my good friend igor in  macedonia believe they believe that it's  just a matter of naturally listening and  reading and you will acquire it i don't  believe that  i think that there has to be a number of  other factors have to come into play  particularly when we're older because as  the child learning  his or her first language it's just  natural they want to communicate they  hear it there's no other language there  patterns are starting to form in the  brain and whatever but when we're older  we have to make a more deliberate effort  we have to want to learn the language we  have to try to  project ourselves we have to want to  communicate with those people all the  things the child does naturally we have  to try a little harder to do we have to  not worry about sounding foolish the  child doesn't care if the child sounds  childish the child is a child we don't  want to sound childish we don't like the  feeling that we're clumsy in another  language so we have to make a greater  effort to overcome these psychological  barriers first of all and the other  thing is that there are things in the  language that even with  the best of intention we don't notice  and i've seen this in my own learning  lots of times so it is helpful to me to  read through a grammar book  i read through it you know leave through  when i start don't intend to remember  anything but just get a general overview  of the language and at various times i  keep up  you know a book around my toilet when i  sit there on the can if you pardon me  leave through it and occasionally you  pick some stuff up but not with the  intention of of learning it so that you  can  you know pass a test on it  all the exercises and quizzes that they  have in those books i ignore them i just  quickly skim over what they have to say  about how the language works  most of which i forget  couldn't possibly remember it doesn't  really make sense  and yet by the fifth or sixth or seventh  time when i read it after a year or two  into the language in fact i'll notice  things even in terms of even though i've  read this thing three times before i  never noticed what they were saying and  then all of a sudden i'll notice it so  and that then helps me to notice it when  i'm listening or reading  in the language  and similarly  these various word lists that we go  through and ideally wordless that come  from our listening and reading reviewing  them in different ways  i like to review words before and after  i read the text and of course link  enables us to do that very easily i like  to occasionally go to my vocabulary  section at lincoln and look words up by  suffix by prefix  alphabetical just different things like  that that help me notice things on a  random basis there is no question in my  mind that the variety of these  activities and some people swear by  their anki and their  space repetition and all these different  things  help us to notice i still believe that  the bulk of our time has to be spent on  exposing it to the exposing us to the  language but to pretend that that it's  just a matter of listening really i  don't buy it i think that's the most  important thing to do we didn't worry  about what we can't remember we need to  try and save it for a test we shouldn't  test kids on language in school  but  we should  as learners and if we have students  encourage them to do things that help  them notice so there i also disagree  with crash  and the third thing and i also don't  agree with him about the idea that  immigrant children should first be learn  in their own language before learning  english i mean  there's lots of hispanic kids so they  might get away with doing it you're  going to do that for everybody it  doesn't make sense to me it makes much  more sense kids move to the united  states get with the program learn  english be as fluent as you possibly can  be in english and then worry about your  spanish when you have time  the other thing is there are people who  still don't believe that a lot of  listening and reading is better than  grammar learning  it is the brain learns i came across  some i was skiing i came across this  russian family and we spoke for a while  in russian i mean my russian was much  better than the parents english they've  been in canada for eight years  no question in my mind  that if those parents had done what i'd  done  spent the amount of time that i've spent  just listening and reading and occasing  flipping occasionally flipping through a  grammar book they would be further ahead  and if they had the determination to do  it  so it works maybe i'm better at it  because i've had ten languages nine  languages whatever but they would be  better off if they did what i did i'm  quite convinced nothing wrong with the  esl school that go there and they make  friends with people from other countries  and it helps them  you know  feel more comfortable in canada but in  terms of language learning if they were  on a program like i was on they would be  miles ahead  that's how they would learn and so they  would learn  and i don't nothing against the term but  i don't like this idea of a dichotomy  between acquisition and learning you  don't learn in school  you learn because the brain
 

